led. It has already run once and reported status `ok`.\n\n**1. What's in the data.** `kibana_sample_data_logs` holds about 14,000 web requests, timestamped 4 Oct to 3 Dec 2026. Each record has:\n- the response code, stored as text: 200 (12,832 requests), 404 (801) and 503 (441) are the only codes present\n- `bytes`, `clientip`, `url` and `request`, `extension` and `host`\n- `agent` and `machine.os`\n- geo fields: `geo.src`, `geo.dest` and coordinates\n\n**2. Dashboard: \"EFF-38818eaf Web logs overview\"** (id `eff-38818eaf-web-logs-overview-08d0a4144a9a`) has seven panels:\n- Total requests\n- Unique visitors (distinct client IPs)\n- Total bytes served\n- Requests over time, split by response code\n- Share of each response code (pie chart)\n- Top 10 URLs, with request count and average bytes\n- Top 10 source countries\n\nMuch of the sample data is dated in the future, so I set the dashboard's default time range to the last 7 days through 60 days ahead (`now-7d` to `now+60d`). That covers the whole dataset.\n\n**3. Alert rule: \"EFF-38818eaf 5xx spike\"** (id `b29329e7-f646-45e4-a860-b6cd03a7d7bb`) is an Elasticsearch query rule on `kibana_sample_data_logs`:\n- It runs every minute.\n- It counts requests whose response code starts with \"5\" in the last 5 minutes, using the `timestamp` field.\n- It fires when that count is more than 10.\n\nThe rule has no actions attached, so when it fires it creates an alert in Kibana but doesn't notify anyone. You'd need to add a connector, such as Slack or email, if you want notifications.","ttft_ms":1845,"type":"result","duration_ms":30340,"uuid":"51bf72fa-4f0c-4702-8856-0c2661cc3099","ttft_stream_ms":1567,"time_to_request_ms":131,"time_to_request_phases_ms":{"other":31,"system_prompt":9,"resume_other":1,"process_user_input":4,"input_attachments":15,"input_hooks":7,"tool_pool_refresh":5,"init_message":4,"engine_pickup":2,"message_prep":2,"model_config_wait":1,"autocompact":3,"query_setup":0,"model_gates":1,"tool_schema_build":20,